  "11-020": "Establishes what the patient understands and acknowledges uncertainty|Explains known and unknown findings in plain language;Listens to concerns and checks understanding|Summarises appropriate options and next steps|Pretending certainty or overwhelming the patient with jargon",
  "12-001": "Introduces weight-management medicines as one part of care|Weighs potential health benefit against adverse effects, cost and ongoing support;Distinguishes products and indications without inventing eligibility rules|Supports evidence-informed individual assessment|Treating medicines as a universal solution or giving prescribing advice",
  "12-002": "Recognises potential need alongside finite NHS resources|Considers effectiveness, cost and equitable access;Includes long-term support and opportunity cost|Gives a conditional view on expansion|Assuming all patients with obesity have identical needs or suitability",
  "12-003": "Proposes clinically relevant criteria for limited access|Considers severity, likely benefit and unmet need;Explains transparent selection and review|Avoids stigma and unjustified social ranking|Prioritising solely by ability to pay or perceived willpower",
  "12-004": "Recognises obesity has biological, behavioural and social influences|Explains interactions between environment and individual choices;Considers access to food, activity and medical support|Shares responsibility without removing individual agency|Reducing obesity to laziness or one cause",
  "12-005": "Identifies ways widespread medication use could change attitudes|Considers reduced blame through recognition of biology;Discusses new appearance pressures or stigma around treatment|Supports nuanced respectful public communication|Assuming medication use will automatically eliminate stigma",
  "12-006": "Frames private access around choice and equity|Discusses affordability, continuity and appropriate oversight;Considers pressure, marketing and resource implications|Supports consistent clinical safeguards across funding routes|Assuming private payment proves a prescription is appropriate",
  "12-007": "Explores the patient's goals and expectations without judgement|Considers wellbeing, suitability and possible pressure about appearance;Explains benefits, harms and alternatives through proper assessment|Maintains prescribing boundaries and supportive care|Prescribing on demand or dismissing appearance-related distress",
  "12-008": "Recognises an influencer's reach can affect medical decisions|Discusses accuracy, conflicts and limits of personal experience;Distinguishes education from promotion and personalised advice|Supports transparency and referral to qualified advice|Presenting a personal success story as universal evidence",
  "12-009": "Distinguishes treatment expenditure from downstream savings|Considers sustained benefit, duration, adverse effects and service costs;Examines uptake and the time horizon of avoided illness|Requests economic evidence rather than assuming savings|Counting benefits while omitting ongoing medication and support costs",
  "12-010": "Identifies a gap between publicity and informed understanding|Explains unrealistic expectations, unsafe sourcing or pressure on services;Considers shortages and misinformation about adverse effects|Suggests clear evidence-based communication and appropriate access|Assuming high demand demonstrates medical value",
  "12-011": "Selects concrete system lessons from the pandemic|Discusses preparedness, workforce protection or communication;Considers unequal impact and continuity of other care|Links lessons to changes that can be evaluated|Treating the pandemic as having one simple lesson",
  "12-012": "Sets out preparation before an outbreak begins|Includes surveillance, workforce capacity and supply resilience;Discusses clear communication and international coordination|Uses exercises and review to test readiness|Focusing only on stockpiles without people or delivery systems",
  "12-013": "Recognises preparedness has value even when unused|Weighs uncertain major harm against ongoing costs;Considers flexible capacity useful between emergencies|Justifies proportionate sustained investment with review|Calling unused preparedness wasted spending by definition",
  "12-014": "Identifies liberty, collective safety and unequal burdens|Examines necessity, proportionality and less restrictive alternatives;Includes transparency, time limits and support for affected people|Reviews restrictions as evidence changes|Treating an emergency as unlimited authority",
  "12-015": "Recognises severe scarcity needs consistent fair processes|Considers urgency and likely benefit with specialist guidance;Rejects social worth and unsupported group assumptions|Includes communication, reassessment and support for all patients|Making arbitrary bedside exclusions based on stereotypes",
  "12-016": "Acknowledges trust changed differently across communities|Considers scientific progress and effective care alongside inconsistent messaging;Discusses misinformation, inequality and changing evidence|Identifies ways to earn trust through openness|Claiming the public had one uniform response",
  "12-017": "Explains why uncertainty created demand for rapid explanations|Discusses emotional stories, information overload and platform incentives;Distinguishes evolving evidence from deliberate deception|Supports accessible timely correction|Calling everyone who shared misinformation unintelligent",
  "12-018": "Finds out which vaccine claim concerns the person|Checks the claim against reliable evidence;Responds respectfully with clear risk explanations|Invites questions and keeps dialogue open|Overwhelming the person with facts before hearing their concern",
  "12-019": "Recognises persistent symptoms can affect daily life and work|Discusses uncertain needs, access and multidisciplinary support;Considers service capacity and research gaps|Supports individualised care without false certainty|Dismissing symptoms because a single explanation is unavailable",
  "12-020": "Listens to the individual's symptoms and priorities|Avoids assuming a uniform long-COVID experience;Considers appropriate assessment, coordinated support and uncertainty|Agrees reviewable goals with the patient|Using a one-size-fits-all care plan or inventing a cure",
  "12-021": "Defines the aims of a targeted versus universal programme|Compares expected benefit, risk, delivery costs and access;Considers changing disease patterns and evidence|Supports a periodically reviewed decision|Presenting current eligibility as permanent or assuming one approach always wins",
  "12-022": "Separates established findings from unresolved questions|Explains why advice may change and what would trigger revision;Uses consistent accessible messages and acknowledges mistakes|Commits to updates and public engagement|Hiding uncertainty to project confidence",
  "12-023": "Chooses a concrete potential use of medical AI|Explains how it could improve workflow, analysis or access;Considers validation, bias and implementation costs|Supports evaluated use with human accountability|Listing futuristic capabilities as proven benefits",
  "12-024": "Identifies a diagnostic risk specific to AI|Discusses unrepresentative data, false outputs or automation bias;Explains validation and monitoring in the intended setting|Keeps clinical assessment and escalation available|Assuming high average accuracy guarantees safety for every patient",
  "12-025": "Treats the AI output as evidence requiring judgement|Checks relevance, reliability and consistency with the patient's situation;Investigates disagreement and seeks appropriate expertise|Takes responsibility for a justified clinical decision|Automatically following or rejecting every AI recommendation",
  "12-026": "Starts with patient support and establishing what happened|Examines clinician, organisation and developer contributions;Distinguishes accountability from unsupported assignment of blame|Supports investigation and preventive change|Blaming the algorithm as if no human or organisation is accountable",
