  "12-032": "Recognises health data require controlled legitimate use|Discusses minimisation, secure access and appropriate governance;Considers re-identification and secondary use|Includes transparency and ongoing oversight|Calling anonymisation an absolute guarantee of privacy",
  "12-033": "Considers what information matters to a patient's decision|Explains the AI's role, limitations and human responsibility;Distinguishes material clinical influence from incidental administration|Supports proportionate understandable transparency|Either concealing meaningful AI involvement or overwhelming patients with technical detail",
  "12-034": "Explains how reduced administration might release consultation time|Considers whether time is used for listening or increased throughput;Discusses accurate records and effects on interaction|Evaluates patient experience rather than assuming improvement|Equating time saved with empathy gained automatically",
  "12-035": "Recognises dependence can affect skill maintenance|Discusses reduced practice, automation bias and loss of independent checking;Includes training and plans for tool failure|Balances useful assistance with preserved competence|Assuming clinicians will retain skills they never exercise",
